1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,760
Today I want to bring your attention to a paradox. Various studies have shown that

2
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:26,280
mental health has been declining steadily for over a decade. This phenomenon is pervasive,

3
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:34,260
it's global, and it's affected younger generations way more than the older generations. On the other

4
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:41,600
hand, we as a society have not been waiting to respond to it. Healthcare companies, governments,

5
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:48,580
organizations have responded valiantly and there's been a huge increase in investments and

6
00:00:48,580 --> 00:00:56,820
efforts to stem this. In U.S. alone, mental health spending has been rising by between three and a

7
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:03,200
half and five percent every year and the latest estimates show that we may have spent as much as

8
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:15,160
280 billion dollars on this problem. Yet, one in five adults continue to suffer. Why is it

9
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:23,720
that this problem continues to rise at a greater pace than all the investments, all the well-intentioned

10
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:31,720
efforts, all the therapies that are out there to address it? I would hypothesize that the reason

11
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:37,540
we are still not able to move the needle is because many of the efforts, the investments,

12
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:46,240
are really in what I would call the fix the person category. Meaning, if you're not feeling very well,

13
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:53,000
perhaps you should log into your app and do some guided meditation or perhaps if it's worse,

14
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:58,420
then maybe you should see a therapist and if you really need help, perhaps we could get you onto a

15
